1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,750
Asha taps Place Order during a sale.

2
00:00:03,575 --> 00:00:06,138
Checkout keeps spinning, then times out.

3
00:00:07,012 --> 00:00:09,625
Her payment may already have succeeded.

4
00:00:11,233 --> 00:00:14,621
Java twenty one virtual threads make blocking waits

5
00:00:14,621 --> 00:00:15,096
cheaper.

6
00:00:15,983 --> 00:00:18,708
They do not make the payment provider faster.

7
00:00:21,233 --> 00:00:24,608
Five hundred waiting requests cannot use a database

8
00:00:24,621 --> 00:00:27,296
pool with twenty connections at once.

9
00:00:28,171 --> 00:00:30,233
The queue simply moves downstream.

10
00:00:32,233 --> 00:00:35,096
First, save one durable payment attempt.

11
00:00:35,996 --> 00:00:38,533
Repeated clicks reuse the same reference.

12
00:00:39,408 --> 00:00:43,058
Never create another charge just because checkout timed

13
00:00:43,058 --> 00:00:43,408
out.

14
00:00:45,033 --> 00:00:47,871
Then bound active calls and waiting work.

15
00:00:48,783 --> 00:00:50,696
A bulkhead protects the provider.

16
00:00:51,596 --> 00:00:54,921
Work not started gets an honest busy response.

17
00:00:57,033 --> 00:00:59,158
Set real network deadlines.

18
00:01:00,033 --> 00:01:03,696
If payment is uncertain, keep the original reference

19
00:01:03,771 --> 00:01:04,783
and reconcile.

20
00:01:05,621 --> 00:01:08,246
A timeout is not proof of cancellation.

21
00:01:10,033 --> 00:01:12,646
Now checkout reports a reliable status.

22
00:01:13,521 --> 00:01:16,496
Virtual threads improve waiting efficiency.

23
00:01:17,358 --> 00:01:20,908
Capacity limits, backpressure and payment safe recovery

24
00:01:20,921 --> 00:01:22,108
protect the customer.
